  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A complex convective setup this afternoon as a pair \nof mid-lvl vorticity centers continue to pivot northeast. One \nsuch area continues to push north of Sioux Falls, with a second \nstronger area of energy now lifting northward through the \nNebraska panhandle. The result thus far has been a significantly \ncontaminated warm sector with plenty of mid-upr clouds over the \nTri-State region. At the surface, a stationary boundary/warm \nfront currently stretches from the Platte River to north of \nOmaha and north of Ames. The airmass north of this boundary \ncontinues to warm, suggesting some potential for a northward \npush in this front by evening as deeper synoptic lift arrives, \nbut for the most part this area will remain capped to surface \nbased convection into late afternoon. \n\nLATE AFTERNOON/EVENING/TONIGHT:  We'll continue to see MLCAPE values \nclimb upwards through the afternoon, potentially between \n2000-3000 J/KG, but how far north with this instability reach is\nstill uncertain. Ongoing elevated convection in NC Nebraska may\nkeep some areas north of I-90 from any significant level of \ndestabilization, with the largest reservoir of CAPE along and \nespecially south of I- 90. Most guidance would favor new \nconvective development along an surface convergence zone in NE \nNebraska or SC South Dakota after 5pm in a narrow corridor of \ndeeper instability. Given favorable shear profiles (effective \nshear 40+ knots), any initial development should have \nsupercellular characteristics capable of 2\" hail and 70 mph \nwinds. Additional convection may try to develop over central \nNebraska and lift northward to the MO River in the evening. As \nwe move through the mid-late evening hours, 0-1km shear will be \non the rise as the LLJ develops, introducing at least the low \nend potential for a brief tornado south of I-90 if storms can \nstay discrete. Though boundary layer stabilization will be on \nthe increase and LCLs remain quite high. Otherwise, watch for \ngradual clustering of storms as they track east northeast into \nMN/IA which would increase the potential of 70+ mph winds, with \na continued very low probability of a QLCS- like tornado into NW\nIowa. 17Z SSCRAM-HRRR data would support the rising potential \nfor high winds extending north of Highway 20, with brief hail \npotential in new updrafts driven by the LLJ further north into \nwestern MN. Latest projections continue to push convection east \nof the CWA by 2am, with light winds and wet ground perhaps \nallowing patch fog to develop. \n\nWEDNESDAY: Brief shortwave ridging tries to move in early Wednesday \nas the surface front settles back into eastern Nebraska and \nnorth central Iowa. A few lingering elevated \nshowers/thunderstorms may persist into NW Iowa through mid-\nmorning. With a slightly drier easterly wind, temperatures again\nrise into the middle to upper 80s, with the latest guidance \nsupporting a strong enough EML to prevent convection along the \nboundary. The return of the nocturnal LLJ Wednesday night could \nagain lead to isolated to scattered elevated convection into \nThursday morning. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: Confidence remains rather high that temperatures \ncontinue to rise towards the lower 90s in most locations for the end \nof the week. Increasing mid-lvl heights through the Plains will help \npush low-lvl temperatures well above normal, with H850 temps +26C \nThursday and Friday.  The greatest challenge in the medium range \nwill be timing and tracking one or more weak ridge riders that will \ncross the Plains into the weekend.  Once such wave may drop down \nfrom the NW into MInnesota early Friday morning, perhaps leaving an \noutflow boundary near the Minnesota River/Ridge areas for the \nafternoon. \n\nSATURDAY-MONDAY:  Temperatures remain above normal through the \nweekend and into early next week as the 500 mb ridge peaks through \nthe central CONUS.  One or more weak waves top the ridge during the \nweekend, creating a very low confidence PoP forecast during the \novernight hours.  Should one of these waves track close enough to \nthe CWA they could lead to some temperature reductions.",
